Raise the simulated gimbal's cycle rate from 5 Hz to 50 Hz.

GZGimbal runs one work item that polls the setpoint, moves the joints, and
publishes GIMBAL_DEVICE_ATTITUDE_STATUS:

    src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp
        ScheduleOnInterval(200_ms); // @5Hz

At 5 Hz the joints step toward a stabilization target five times a second,
so the camera visibly lags the airframe during any maneuver, and the
attitude report is up to 200 ms of joint motion stale. Every consumer that
projects through the camera frame inherits that lag: image overlays swim
against the video while the gimbal stabilizes.

At 50 Hz the joints follow a streamed setpoint within 20 ms, which is
below one video frame, and the report keeps the TF tree honest at the
same cadence. The work item does little per cycle (a few uORB reads and
gz topic publishes; the device info publish is request-gated), so the
extra rate costs nothing measurable.

Pair this with a mavlink stream rate for the report, or mavros still
receives it at the profile default:

    mavlink stream -r 50 -s GIMBAL_DEVICE_ATTITUDE_STATUS -u <mavros udp port>

px4-sim-stack applies this patch and sets that stream in px4-rcS.

    cd src/PX4-Autopilot
    git apply ../../patches/px4-gzgimbal-rate.patch

--- a/src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp
+++ b/src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp
@@ -52,7 +52,7 @@

 	updateParameters();

-	ScheduleOnInterval(200_ms); // @5Hz
+	ScheduleOnInterval(20_ms); // @50Hz

 	// Schedule on vehicle command messages
 	if (!_vehicle_command_sub.registerCallback()) {
